# Quartz Integration & Content Rendering Strategy

Integrate **Quartz** (`https://quartz.jzhao.xyz/`) into the portfolio repository to render blog posts, notes, and content collections directly from the `content/` Obsidian vault with rich digital garden capabilities (Interactive Graph View, Backlinks, Popover Page Previews, Wikilink Resolution, Explorer Tree, and Obsidian Callouts).

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Architectural Approaches for Quartz Integration**
> 
> Quartz (created by Jacky Zhao) is designed to transform Obsidian vaults into interactive digital gardens. Because our content directory (`content/`) is structured as an Obsidian vault (containing `.base` files, wikilinks, and callouts), there are two main architectural patterns for integrating Quartz:
> 
> 1. **Option A: Next.js + Quartz Component & Parsing Pipeline (Recommended)**
>    - Incorporate Quartz's core components (Interactive Graph View, Backlink indexer, Popover Page Previews, Wikilink resolver) directly into our Next.js 15 App Router (`lib/markdown.ts`, `app/blog/[pageId]/page.tsx`).
>    - **Pros**: Retains all portfolio features (Tailwind v4 theme system, dynamic Redis like/view counters, newsletter subscriptions, rate-limiting, layout navbar/footer, fast server-side rendering).
>    - **Cons**: Requires building custom React components for Graph View and Popovers based on Quartz client scripts.
> 
> 2. **Option B: Quartz 4 CLI Build Target (`npx quartz build`)**
>    - Install and configure Quartz 4 (`@jackyzha0/quartz`) directly in the workspace, generating static HTML files into `public/notes` or output directory during build.
>    - **Pros**: Zero-config Quartz standard UI out-of-the-box, matching standard `jzhao.xyz` layout.
>    - **Cons**: Quartz operates outside Next.js App Router, requiring sub-domain or static path hosting (`/notes`), and does not natively share our React navbar/footer or Redis backend APIs.
> 
> **Recommendation**: Option A offers a seamless user experience where the entire blog functions as a full-featured Quartz digital garden while maintaining the unified Next.js portfolio design and dynamic functionality.

